  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q24-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Prove that from any collection of $n$ integers, we can choose a non-empty sub-collection whose sum is divisible by $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Consider the $n$ partial sums modulo $n$.\n\nEither one has remainder 0, or two have the same remainder; their difference is the sum of a non-empty sub-collection.",
      "worked": "Label the integers $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$ and define\n$$s_k=a_1+\\cdots+a_k\\qquad(1\\le k\\le n).$$\nIf some $s_k\\equiv0\\pmod n$, the first $k$ integers give the required sub-collection.\nOtherwise the $n$ partial sums have only $n-1$ possible non-zero remainders. By the pigeonhole principle, $s_i\\equiv s_j\\pmod n$ for some $i<j$.\n$$\\begin{align*}s_j-s_i&=a_{i+1}+\\cdots+a_j\\\\s_j-s_i&\\equiv0\\pmod n.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $i<j$, this sub-collection is non-empty.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 75
  },
